WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:06.160
Intelligent.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:07.610
Indestructible.

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:09.700
And with no humans on board,

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.420
these sail boats are plotting

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:15.133
their own course through the
waters of San Francisco Bay.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:19.400
If this guy gets his way,

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.380
soon there will be hundreds of them

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:23.541
trawling the ocean for data.

00:00:23.541 --> 00:00:26.458
(electronic music)

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:35.320
This is Richard Jenkins.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.403
And he's someone who likes a challenge.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.610
Here he is trying to
break the world record

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:45.920
for land sailing in 2009.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:48.560
I registered at 116 miles an hour,

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.340
which I thought was kind of a low bar

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.060
and I could do it in maybe a year

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:54.490
and it was actually anything but easy.

00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:56.490
Instead of one year, it took Jenkins

00:00:56.490 --> 00:01:00.030
a decade of toiling in
deserts around the world.

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:01.890
But break the record he did,

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.350
clocking just over 126 miles per hour

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:07.573
in his own custom designed vehicle.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.490
The next challenge was a nod to Magellan

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.214
with a modern twist.

00:01:13.214 --> 00:01:14.427
It encouraged me that
nothing has ever gone

00:01:14.427 --> 00:01:16.910
around the world unmanned
and it's kind of the last

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:18.780
great records to get.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.670
So Jenkins set out to
build a robotic sailboat

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:24.750
capable of circumnavigating the globe.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:28.370
In 2013, he sent the world's
first unmanned vessel

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:32.400
across an ocean, going from
San Francisco to Hawaii.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:33.890
We actually called it the Honey Badger

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:34.950
because, you know, it doesn't give a shit,

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:36.720
it just plows through anything.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.830
And somewhere, amidst all that,

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:41.023
he found the time to start a new business.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:51.600
Based in this former aircraft
hangar, in Alameda, California

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.620
Jenkin's company Saildrone
has already raised

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:57.203
90 million dollars from investors.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:03.770
And here's what's got those
venture capitalists so excited.

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:08.220
A 20 strong fleet of
autonomous sea-worthy drones.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:10.916
Who put the shark faces on them?

00:02:10.916 --> 00:02:13.516
That was me, last weekend,
I just freehanded that.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:17.280
Powered only by the wind and sun,

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.810
these GPS guided bots can survive

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.623
at sea for months at a time.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.500
Bristling with sensors,
scientific equipment,

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.470
and cameras, they beam back real time data

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:31.930
and images via satellite
from the ocean surface.

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:33.310
These two vehicles are
on their way back from

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:35.380
a seven, almost eight month voyage.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:36.970
Probably 10 thousand miles.

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:37.890
Where did they go?

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:39.340
Down to the Equatorial Pacific,

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:41.280
studying El Nino affects on the equator.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:42.410
Right.

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:43.480
You wanna know what it looks like?

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:44.791
Is this the camera view?

00:02:44.791 --> 00:02:46.653
Yes, this is a real time camera view.

00:02:46.653 --> 00:02:48.561
And there it is, that's a picture from

00:02:48.561 --> 00:02:50.084
Equatorial Pacific right now.

00:02:50.084 --> 00:02:51.153
It's nice and sunny.

00:02:51.153 --> 00:02:52.183
It's always sunny.

00:02:52.183 --> 00:02:53.633
It's always sunny down there.

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:01.760
Right now, Saildrone is busy

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.000
prepping for a new mission.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.010
The 2000 mile round trip
to an area of Pacific Ocean

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.093
known as the white shark cafe.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:14.490
Great White's come to this
remote spot to hang out

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:17.761
for months at a time and
scientists have spent decades

00:03:17.761 --> 00:03:20.030
wanting to know why.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:21.310
So we're working with Stanford University

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:24.200
to take these vehicles and
literally follow the sharks

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:26.210
to the cafe to understand, literally,

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:27.043
what they're doing there.

00:03:27.043 --> 00:03:28.880
Is it food, is it reproduction?

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:30.490
No one really knows.

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:33.670
The drones are ideal
for missions like this.

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:36.370
They're much cheaper
than large research ships

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:39.230
and can collect many
different types of data.

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:42.140
PH, chlorophyll, humidity, radiation,

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:43.950
wind speed, direction, temperature,

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:44.783
the list goes on.

00:03:44.783 --> 00:03:47.080
We measure everything you
could possibly measure

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:48.580
near the surface of the ocean.

00:03:50.707 --> 00:03:52.590
And data like this sheds light

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:54.340
on something that affects everyone.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:57.873
The weather.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.210
There really is infinite use for

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:04.480
more accurate weather and climate data.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:07.618
So a big focus for us this
year is deploying drones

00:04:07.618 --> 00:04:10.990
into the hurricane field so
we can find out how strong

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:13.080
they're going to be and
where they're going to land.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:15.450
And the impacts of that
information is huge.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:18.180
So for insurance risk, financial markets,

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.180
and for people's safety.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:22.810
On an unmanned craft,
hurricane force winds

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:24.713
would shred a conventional sail.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:30.843
So instead, the drones harness
the wind using the hard

00:04:30.843 --> 00:04:34.623
vertical wing adapted from
Jenkins' land sailing days.

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:39.440
When wind passes over an
airplane wing, it produces lift.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:42.424
In this case, the wind
produces thrust that moves

00:04:42.424 --> 00:04:44.053
the sail drone forward.

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:48.080
Jenkins invented a tail,
with a small adjustable tab

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:49.890
to control this force.

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:52.184
It stops the wing from
spinning out of control

00:04:52.184 --> 00:04:55.880
and lets the robot make
snap sailing decisions.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.340
Everything about the craft is 100 percent

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:00.553
salt-water proof and submersible.

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:08.070
Today I'm hitching a
ride to see Rich deploy

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:10.793
two of his drones to the white shark cafe.

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:16.510
There's not much wind
so they're given a tow

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:19.930
into San Francisco Bay
before being released.

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:21.512
There's enough wind here
now we can start sailing.

00:05:21.512 --> 00:05:22.910
We just left the city and this is where

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:24.810
we normally let them go.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:25.643
Goodbye drone.

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:29.673
As soon as their released,
they're on full autonomous mode.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:34.010
You guys have gone 200
thousand nautical miles

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:35.360
without an incident?

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:38.770
Yep, we've had the drones
operating for four years now.

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:40.840
And not a single scratch on the vehicle.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:42.993
And nobody's every phoned
it in as a, like a UFO

00:05:42.993 --> 00:05:45.510
or some sailor's like what
the hell are these things

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:46.343
doing out in the water?

00:05:46.343 --> 00:05:48.722
No, they behave really predictably

00:05:48.722 --> 00:05:50.910
and safely around ships.

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:53.480
And there are animals that
come up and check them out?

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:54.313
Yep.

00:05:54.313 --> 00:05:55.146
We got some really good pictures.

00:05:55.146 --> 00:05:58.490
So, there's a spotted fur
seal, jumped on for a ride

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:01.272
up in the Chukchi sea
about 65 degrees north,

00:06:01.272 --> 00:06:03.503
and hung out there for about six hours.

00:06:04.872 --> 00:06:08.372
(electronic bubbly music)

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:15.130
These drones have a two
week journey ahead of them

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:17.416
before they make their shark rendezvous

00:06:17.416 --> 00:06:20.730
and soon, they'll be part
of an even bigger fleet.

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:22.220
I want to get a thousand drones

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:23.257
within three to four years.

00:06:23.257 --> 00:06:25.610
And that gives us full global coverage

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:27.630
so we have as much or
better data of oceans

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:28.780
that we currently have for land

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:31.897
and that will transform our
understanding of our planet.

00:06:37.986 --> 00:06:41.319
(electronic fading out)

